00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:50.630
to confidently build and
deploy a virtual assistant.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.380
So during our evaluation
period, our primary goal

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.630
was to measure the performance
of intent detection.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:56.890
So what's an intent?

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.638
Quite simply, a user
question or intention.

00:09:59.638 --> 00:10:01.930
And really, it's the first
and the key step to building

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.515
a virtual assistant.

00:10:03.515 --> 00:10:05.890
If you break down a virtual
assistant into two high level

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.808
components, it's detecting
what the user wants

00:10:08.808 --> 00:10:10.350
and helping them
fulfill that intent,

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:12.340
or what's known
as a fulfillment.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:13.840
Now we actually
have a separate team

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.007
that manages more of the
fulfillment side of things.

00:10:16.007 --> 00:10:18.730
And that's more procedural
nature, where you're basically

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:21.820
going out to fetch user data or
performing an action on behalf

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:22.860
of the user.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.370
Where the intent
detection side is

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:27.100
where more of the AI and machine
learning comes into play.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:28.870
And that's what my
team focuses on,

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.412
and that's going to be really
the theme around my talk today.

00:10:33.468 --> 00:10:35.510
So for those who aren't
familiar with Dialogflow,

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.510
let me explain
some of the basics.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:42.650
A Dialogflow agent is a
logical representation

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:44.420
of a virtual assistant.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:46.460
To your agent, you add
a number of intents.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:49.935
And then to each intent, you
add a set of training phrases.

00:10:49.935 --> 00:10:51.560
The training phrases
are then basically

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.570
used to customize
your model that's

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:56.150
really built on top of
Google's underlying NLU models.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:58.628
They're built from
years of experience.

00:10:58.628 --> 00:11:00.170
So your training
phases really should

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:01.837
include examples of
how a customer might

00:11:01.837 --> 00:11:03.800
phrase a specific question.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:08.120
For example here, I have an
intent called available credit,

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:09.730
where a customer's asking--

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.380
their intent would be inquiring
about their available credit

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:14.065
to spend, is what it is.

00:11:14.065 --> 00:11:15.440
And yoy can get
some of this data

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.600
from actual customer messages
that you would either sanitize

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.110
or anonymize as part
of your input to this.

00:11:23.595 --> 00:11:25.970
One of the important features
of Dialogflow that we liked

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:27.950
was something called confidence.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.080
Essentially with
each intent detection

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:32.708
that Dialogflow performs,
it returns you back a score

00:11:32.708 --> 00:11:35.000
to indicate how confident it
was in that intent it just

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.070
detected.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:39.250
And this was really
important to us

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:41.030
to be able to make
intelligent decisions

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:44.090
about our virtual
assistant replies.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.700
Dialogflow allows you to
set a minimum threshold,

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.520
that if this confidence score
does not meet that threshold,

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.290
they can do one of two things--

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:55.515
return you back a fallback
intent or no intent at all.

00:11:55.515 --> 00:11:57.140
A fallback intent
can be something just

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:58.932
like a clarifying
statement asking the user

00:11:58.932 --> 00:12:01.640
to reword their question,
but this is something

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.860
that we decided not to
implement because really, it

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:06.027
was more important for us
to be able to get it right

00:12:06.027 --> 00:12:07.412
and try to automate it.

00:12:07.412 --> 00:12:09.620
And if you actually notice
here, something that we do

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:13.275
is we set our minimum threshold
extremely low, just 10%.

00:12:13.275 --> 00:12:14.900
Now it doesn't mean
that we're actually

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.923
sending the replies back to
users in all the scenarios,

00:12:17.923 --> 00:12:20.090
but what we're doing is
we're collecting data around

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:21.673
on the intent detection
process, so we

00:12:21.673 --> 00:12:24.173
can make more informed decisions
about how we want to really

00:12:24.173 --> 00:12:25.242
send our replies back.

00:12:25.242 --> 00:12:27.200
And I'll touch on this
a little bit more later.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:32.670
So the testing process
that we established

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.440
during our evaluation
period is something

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:36.810
that we've actually built
into our daily process

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.900
as we're adding new intents and
we're updating existing ones.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:43.650
To start off with, you
have to scour for real data

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:47.460
from customers, whether it
be from existing IVR channel,

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.510
chat, or it could even
be inbound email channel.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:52.470
But you might really have to
spend some time to actually get

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:53.693
good labeled data.

00:12:53.693 --> 00:12:55.860
So here's a customer message,
and here's what intent

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.690
you expect to get back.

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:59.237
And something to
know is you want

00:12:59.237 --> 00:13:01.320
to make sure that your
training data that you used

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.570
for the train phase is
separate from your testing data

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:06.330
because you don't want your
results to become biased.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:07.920
So when you have
your labeled data

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.810
with a message
and an intent, you

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:11.580
can send it through
the Dialogflow's API

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:13.230
to detect the intent.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.720
You get back a name of an
intent, along with a confidence

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:16.620
score.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:18.870
And then from there, you
compare it to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:22.668
and expected intent, and you can
generate a set of core metrics.

00:13:22.668 --> 00:13:24.210
Now we do this on
a per intent basis.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.252
Essentially for each intent
that we have defined,

00:13:26.252 --> 00:13:29.980
we go through and calculate
each one of these metrics.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.490
So giving a couple examples,
if a customer said,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.880
why is my credit showing
at $0, if we detected this

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.000
as the intent available
credit, and we

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.170
expected to get
available credit,

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.750
then while we're calculating
available credit metrics,

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:45.182
we consider this
a true positive.

00:13:45.182 --> 00:13:46.890
However, if we're
calculating the metrics

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.250
for another intent
called a payment posting,

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:51.570
we consider this
a true negative.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:54.320
Because we did not expect to
get the intent payment posting,

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.730
and we did not get back
the intent payment posting.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:59.700
And that's the definition
of what a true negative is.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.670
For the purposes of our
individual intent metrics,

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.935
this is not as important,
but we still notate it--

00:14:05.935 --> 00:14:09.060
the true negative, that is.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.310
Let's say for another
message a customer said,

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:14.130
it shows my payment posted,
but I have no credit available.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:17.190
Just wondering when
it will be available.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:20.250
If Dialogflow detected
this as payment posting,

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:23.070
but we expected to get the
intent available credit,

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:25.320
then while we're calculating
available credit metrics,

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.015
we consider this
a false negative.

00:14:27.015 --> 00:14:28.980
But conversely, for
payment posting,

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.750
we consider this
a false positive.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.360
So once we're done
with this process,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.360
we then calculate a set
of more advanced metrics

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:37.416
that can give us
some better insights.

00:14:37.416 --> 00:14:39.400
The first one is
known as precision,

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.705
or positive predictive value.

00:14:41.705 --> 00:14:43.330
We can use this to
determine if we have

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:45.200
problems with false positives.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.650
And really, it's defined as
the total number of tests

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:49.600
where a specific
intent was detected.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:50.957
For example, available credit.

00:14:50.957 --> 00:14:52.540
What percentage of
those were correct?

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:58.010
Another intent known as
recall or sensitivity

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:00.620
or true positive
rate can be defined

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:02.480
as the total number
of tests where

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.440
a specific intent was expected.

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:06.530
For example, available credit.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.660
What percentage of those
were detected correctly

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:10.830
as available credit?

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.650
And this will let us know if our
intent is too narrowly defined,

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:16.780
missing messages that
it's intended to pick up.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:20.200
So you're using our simple data
set from the previous slide.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.370
We would end up with
a precision of 100%,

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.675
but a recall of only 50%
for available credit.

00:15:25.675 --> 00:15:28.300
So really, these two metrics are
complementary for the purposes

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:30.520
of measuring performance.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:33.470
Precision can be 100% with
only one true positive

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.360
if we had no false positives.

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:37.208
But if recall isl low
for the same intent,

00:15:37.208 --> 00:15:39.625
it really means it's missing
almost all messages entirely.

00:15:39.625 --> 00:15:42.365
It doesn't really
provide much value.

00:15:42.365 --> 00:15:44.990
Recently we've actually started
calculating a new metric called

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:48.420
F1 score, which is the harmonic
average of both precision

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:49.665
and recall.

00:15:49.665 --> 00:15:51.540
As the number of intents
has grown and became

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:53.748
more cumbersome to look at
so many different metrics,

00:15:53.748 --> 00:15:55.830
so the F1 score
provides a single number

00:15:55.830 --> 00:16:00.860
that we can look at to look
at our intent performance.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:03.200
So comparing a couple intents
seems easy enough, right?

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:06.075
But what happens when you
scale to 10, 50, 100 intents?

00:16:06.075 --> 00:16:08.075
This is where a tool known
as a confusion matrix

00:16:08.075 --> 00:16:10.250
can really come in handy.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:12.493
Using the output data
from our test process,

00:16:12.493 --> 00:16:14.660
we can generate essentially
a heat map of the intent

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:16.790
detection performance.

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:18.770
We map the detected
intent along the rows

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:20.660
and the expected
intent in the columns.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:22.700
And in each cell is
the percentage of times

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:24.870
where those intents meet.

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.560
So if you see the dark line
from the top left diagonal down

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:29.180
the middle to the
bottom right, this

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:32.030
is where the detected and
the expected intents match.

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:33.560
And this is what we want to see.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:36.310
Basically, the lighter
shade means less confusion,

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:37.900
or less times they meet.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:40.590
And a darker shade you really
want to see in the middle.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:44.060
So anytime we see a darker
shade outside of that middle

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.680
is where we have
areas of opportunity.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.600
So for example here,
I've highlighted

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.840
an intent called unrecognized
transaction that's

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:54.470
been detected 19% of the
time when we really expect

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.730
to get pending transaction.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.408
So what can we do
when we see this?

00:16:58.408 --> 00:16:59.700
There's a few different things.

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:01.190
One of them is we need to
go down into the training

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:04.079
phases for each of these intents
and look for similarities

00:17:04.079 --> 00:17:05.740
that we can try to eliminate.

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:07.240
Second thing that
we could try to do

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.073
is actually adjust the
confidence threshold.

00:17:09.073 --> 00:17:10.510
Perhaps if we
increased it, there

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:14.380
could be less false positives
for unrecognized transaction.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:17.290
Lastly, you might just need
to combine these two intents

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:19.270
into a single intent
and then build

00:17:19.270 --> 00:17:20.770
the conversation
flows out to direct

00:17:20.770 --> 00:17:21.978
the user down the right path.

00:17:24.968 --> 00:17:27.260
So now I'll touch a little
bit on the Dialogflow agents

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:28.610
that we use.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:30.770
Similar to common software
engineering practices

00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:33.260
where you have different
development test production

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.600
environments, we do
a similar structure

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:37.187
for our Dialogflow agents.

00:17:37.187 --> 00:17:39.020
And our development and
training environment

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:41.300
is where all of our
updates happen manually

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:44.660
either via the Dialogflow
console or via some processes

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.060
that we built out via API
calls that they provide

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:49.333
to update the training phrases.

00:17:49.333 --> 00:17:51.500
When we're ready to promote
to the next environment,

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:53.390
we can execute a
deployment pipeline

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:56.235
that exports from development
to our staging environment.

00:17:56.235 --> 00:17:57.860
Here, we can perform
acceptance testing

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:00.680
and run some regression
tests before confident enough

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:03.380
to deploy to production,
where we actually extract out

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:05.120
the entire configuration
into an artifact

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.733
that we've sent into
artifact repository.

00:18:07.733 --> 00:18:09.650
And we execute another
pipeline to deploy that

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:11.300
into our production environment.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:13.758
There's never any manual updates
happening into production.

00:18:13.758 --> 00:18:16.130
It's all through processes
that are automated.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:19.110
Being a finance institution,
we're highly regulated.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:21.380
Having auditable processes
that are repeatable

00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:22.541
is really important.

00:18:24.827 --> 00:18:26.910
So expanding a little bit
upon the pipeline I just

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:29.285
talked about, this is something
that our business product

00:18:29.285 --> 00:18:32.298
owners can initiate
and manage themselves.

00:18:32.298 --> 00:18:34.590
It runs through all the tests
and the metric generation

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:36.030
that I've talked about.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.280
And it compares the metrics
against the prior version.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.310
So you can actually see
where things have improved.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:44.040
Or you may have
introduced some issues.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:46.700
And then it actually generates
a summary report for you

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.367
that will highlight
the changes for you,

00:18:48.367 --> 00:18:50.380
instead of having to
go search for them.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:53.560
So while the engineering team
has built out this process,

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:56.160
It's something that a business
or product owner can easily

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.097
execute and interpret
the results themselves.

00:18:59.097 --> 00:19:01.305
So now I'd like to give a
quick demo of this process.

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:08.450
And full disclosure here,
this is a pre-recorded demo.

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:10.710
I want to be risk averse
and also cut out a couple

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.840
minutes from the middle.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:16.080
So right now, I'm logged
into the Dialogflow web

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:19.160
console here, updating
our training agent.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.342
And I'm editing the
intent, available credit.

00:19:22.342 --> 00:19:23.800
Here's the training
phrase section.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.342
So what I'm actually
going to do here

00:19:25.342 --> 00:19:28.050
is the phrase that we saw
earlier, my example that got

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:29.887
detected as payment
posting, even though I'm

00:19:29.887 --> 00:19:31.470
going against my own
advice, I'm going

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:33.060
to go ahead and add this
as a training phase,

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:34.578
knowing that it's
in a testing set.

00:19:34.578 --> 00:19:36.370
And I should see a
performance improvement,

00:19:36.370 --> 00:19:36.840
I would hope, right?

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:38.220
So let's go ahead and add that.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:39.770
We'll save the changes.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:42.438
I'm going to hop over
to another tab here.

00:19:42.438 --> 00:19:43.980
This is actually an
internal web tool

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:46.147
that we've built out for
our business product owners

00:19:46.147 --> 00:19:47.790
to help manage our
virtual assistant.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:49.990
They can do a number of
things from this tool.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:54.295
And one of them is actually
run the deployment pipeline.

00:19:54.295 --> 00:19:54.795
Oops.

00:19:57.662 --> 00:19:59.912
So here, we're going to go
ahead and select the source

00:19:59.912 --> 00:20:01.340
as the training agent.

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:04.072
And we're going to deploy
that to our staging agent.

00:20:04.072 --> 00:20:05.780
And once we kick off
the deployment here,

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:09.740
we get a message saying
our job has started.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:13.603
I'm going to hop over to our
continuous integration server,

00:20:13.603 --> 00:20:15.770
and just go ahead and explain
some of the steps that

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:17.768
are going on here.

00:20:17.768 --> 00:20:19.310
So one of the first
things that we do

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:21.230
is we export the source agent.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:23.150
Dialogflow provides
an API we can actually

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:25.640
extract the entire
configuration into a zip.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:28.310
So we do that for the
source and the target.

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:30.650
And then we perform
some validation tests.

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:33.160
This is mostly just to
look at the content.

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:35.930
Dialogflow provides a
capability to set up

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:38.120
a custom payload
for your responses,

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.310
that we use dynamic
slots for user data.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.310
So we're just doing some
schema validation there

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:44.813
to make sure it's well formed.

00:20:44.813 --> 00:20:46.730
The next thing that we
do is actually generate

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:47.760
a difference report.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:49.930
So basically, any
intents that were added,

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:52.240
anything where training
phases were updated

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:53.810
or any content
that's changed, we're

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:55.790
going to list out all the
changes that have happened.

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:57.410
Sometimes if there's
multiple people that

00:20:57.410 --> 00:20:59.180
are updating the agent,
we want to make sure

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:00.555
that we list out
all the changes,

00:21:00.555 --> 00:21:03.672
and we have that documented.

00:21:03.672 --> 00:21:05.630
Next thing that we do is
think about those unit

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:07.002
tests in software engineering.

00:21:07.002 --> 00:21:08.710
Certain phrases we
want to make sure that

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:11.180
are always detected
as specific intents,

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:12.710
so we make sure that those pass.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.677
If not, they're going
to fail the deployment.

00:21:14.677 --> 00:21:16.760
Next thing that we do is
run through all the tests

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:18.570
and metrics that
I've talked about.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:21.473
And then we actually perform
a threshold validation,

00:21:21.473 --> 00:21:23.390
making sure that all our
metrics at least meet

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:27.220
a minimum standard, or else,
we'll fail the deployment.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.740
So after that's done, we'll
get a Slack notification

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:31.048
to our team channel.

00:21:31.048 --> 00:21:33.340
Here our product owners can
then go ahead and check out

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:35.845
the results.

00:21:35.845 --> 00:21:37.970
We send a link that they
can go ahead and download.

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:39.890
And we actually
require an approval,

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:42.640
so obviously, we want to
check the results out first

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:45.730
before they approve
the actual deployment.

00:21:45.730 --> 00:21:48.160
So here, I'm going to go
download our difference report

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:50.770
and hopefully that we see
just one change was made.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.408
And here, we can see intent
changed, available credit,

00:21:56.408 --> 00:21:58.200
and here's the training
phase that I added.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:00.358
So that's good.

00:22:00.358 --> 00:22:02.150
Now we'll go into our
test evidence folder.

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:04.010
Here, we can see there's
a confusion matrix.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.990
There's the test
metrics and the raw test

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:07.573
results, every single
message and what

00:22:07.573 --> 00:22:09.600
it was detected as if we
want to investigate it.

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:11.720
Not that we do it every
time, but it's there.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:14.095
So I'm going to go ahead and
download the summary report.

00:22:14.095 --> 00:22:16.280
And here we can see,
OK, the precision

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:17.570
increased for payment posting.

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:20.346
That's good, less
false positives there.

00:22:20.346 --> 00:22:22.070
But it looks like the
precision decreased

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:25.220
for a couple of other intents
that it wasn't expecting.

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:27.060
The recall did go up
for available credit.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:28.730
So that's good.

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:30.770
True positives went up there.

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:32.180
But the recall
actually went down

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:33.430
for a number of other intents.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.450
So it's pretty interesting.

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:36.950
Really, kind of
what this highlights

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:40.400
is machine learning AI's
not really an exact science.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:42.610
You can't always expect
something's going

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:43.922
to work out the way it is.

00:22:43.922 --> 00:22:45.380
And just having a
process like this

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:46.980
that we can automate
really helps us

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:48.230
to enable to do this at scale.

00:22:50.202 --> 00:22:51.160
OK, you can click back.

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.370
All right, so now that we've
deployed to production,

00:22:58.370 --> 00:22:59.750
how do we monitor
our performance

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.670
and continue to improve it?

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:04.550
So within our internal
web tool, we've

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:07.730
actually built a process
where we can view the messages

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:09.313
and what they were detected as.

00:23:09.313 --> 00:23:10.730
We can go ahead
and annotate those

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:12.620
as either correct or incorrect.

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:15.130
And if they were wrong,
what was the right intent?

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:17.213
This allows us to do a
couple of different things.

00:23:17.213 --> 00:23:19.547
One of them is actually
generate the performance metrics

00:23:19.547 --> 00:23:21.047
of our real production
model, right?

00:23:21.047 --> 00:23:23.505
It's one thing to be able to
look at in a test environment,

00:23:23.505 --> 00:23:25.120
saying, OK, this is
how we should do.

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:27.370
But it's much more valuable
to see how you're actually

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:28.990
doing in production.

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:30.653
Second thing that
we do is actually

00:23:30.653 --> 00:23:32.070
create an automated
process, where

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:33.760
we can export some
of these messages

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.170
into our test environment.

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:38.110
Let's say a message was detected
at a very little confidence

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:40.180
threshold, but that
was the correct intent.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:42.430
We can go ahead and try to
add that to our development

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:45.675
environment to see how it
improves the performance.

00:23:45.675 --> 00:23:47.050
Lastly, we can
generate something

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:48.640
that's known as an ROC curve.

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:50.140
Here we plot the
false positive rate

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:52.330
along the x-axis and
the true positive rate

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:55.910
along the y-axis for varying
confidence thresholds.

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:57.790
So for example here,
I've highlighted

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:00.310
at an 80% confidence
minimum threshold,

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:02.980
I can expect to get about
a 3.9% false positive rate

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:06.470
and a [INAUDIBLE]
true positive rate.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.620
So circling back to
my previous slide

00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:11.830
in confidence, if we had our
minimum threshold set at 80%,

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:14.390
we wouldn't be able to collect
any of this data to the right.

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:17.235
So by setting our confidence
low, collecting the data,

00:24:17.235 --> 00:24:19.360
we can analyze it and make
a more informed decision

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:21.550
about how we want to set
our confidence thresholds.

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:23.133
Otherwise, you're
just taking a guess.

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:27.730
So talking a little
bit about how

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:30.460
we utilize this data in
production, our high level

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:31.150
process--

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:33.233
basically, when we're
receiving incoming messages,

00:24:33.233 --> 00:24:35.530
we first go to Dialogflow,
detect the intent,

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:37.930
and get back a confidence score.

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:39.940
Part of our business
logic is then, one,

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:42.490
looking to see if we had
that intent enabled, and two,

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:45.663
if it meets the minimum
conference threshold.

00:24:45.663 --> 00:24:47.080
We enable the
conference threshold

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:49.180
to be set on a
per attempt basis.

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:52.153
And if one's not set,
we use a global default.

00:24:52.153 --> 00:24:54.070
So now you might be
asking, why would you have

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:56.170
an intent enabled or disabled?

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:58.730
Why are you going to have
that in your Dialogflow agent?

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:01.060
And there's a number of
different reasons for this.

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:02.940
One of them is if you
have something deployed

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:04.500
and you see an
issue with it, being

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:07.873
able to quickly turn it off is
something that is useful to us.

00:25:07.873 --> 00:25:10.290
You can say, why not just
delete the intent of Dialogflow?

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:12.750
But as we've seen, making
updates to your model

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:14.910
can have unintended
consequences.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:16.410
Second thing that
it allows us to do

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:18.438
is actually deploy new
intents to a smaller

00:25:18.438 --> 00:25:19.230
number of channels.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:20.813
We can actually
control these settings

00:25:20.813 --> 00:25:22.750
at a per channel basis.

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:25.900
So we have a channel where
only employees can access.

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:28.570
So sometimes we'll beta test
the new intent in a channel

00:25:28.570 --> 00:25:30.820
where only employees will
see it before rolling it out

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:32.900
to the broader public.

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:34.650
And the last thing
that we can actually do

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.870
is define intents
that we're either not

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:38.730
ready to fulfill yet,
or we just don't have

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:40.860
an intent desire to fulfill.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:44.280
This can deflect away from
giving incorrect responses.

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:47.970
One example might be
for an intent increasing

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:49.230
the user's credit limit.

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:51.480
Let's say that somebody comes
in, and we see them ask,

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:53.370
I want to decrease
my credit limit.

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:55.390
I mean, this isn't
a common request.

00:25:55.390 --> 00:26:00.320
So if this was detected as the
increase credit limit intent,

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:02.742
it's going to end up with
a bad user experience.

00:26:02.742 --> 00:26:04.200
So by quickly
defining a new intent

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:05.908
to decrease credit
limit, we can go ahead

00:26:05.908 --> 00:26:08.640
and get that detected, but
then route it to an agent

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:11.750
to handle it so it has a
better customer experience.

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:14.000
And with that, I'll go ahead
and turn it over to Price

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:15.228
to wrap things up here.

00:26:15.228 --> 00:26:18.574
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:24.200
PRICE VETTER: So yeah, just
some of our key takeaways,

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:26.720
some of our learnings
over the past year--

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:28.220
when we started
this process, it was

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:33.800
really we needed to tackle kind
of the preconceived notions

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:39.590
that people think of when
AI is brought to bear.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:43.490
So tackle this head on,
bring examples of what

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:45.140
AI is in their everyday lives.

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:47.660
And that will really
help people understand

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:52.220
that it's not this monster
under the bed kind of thing.

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:55.340
When we defined
our processes for--

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:58.640
taking in intents and really
defining the customer value,

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:01.700
the value for us as a
business, and all the way

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:04.130
through production
into post-production,

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:09.430
and being able to
train and improve

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:12.710
that experience post-launch.

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:14.690
We really didn't
reinvent the wheel there.

00:27:14.690 --> 00:27:17.090
We leaned on existing processes.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:21.050
We used metrics
from other channels,

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:24.980
so that when we were talking
about the virtual assistant,

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:28.460
it wasn't confusing
everybody in the room.

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:31.040
Big for us-- be
customer focused.

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.730
And John mentioned this earlier.

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:37.040
We were able to introduce
new intents that were not

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:42.620
necessarily fulfilling to get
the customer to a customer

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:44.720
service expert as
fast as possible

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.960
and not get them stuck in this
loop of, did you mean this?

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:48.770
Did you mean that?

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:51.560
Just really trying to
provide the best customer

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:56.150
experience possible is
really what has helped us

00:27:56.150 --> 00:27:57.690
through this process.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:00.050
And then the last two
kind of go together.

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:05.540
Enabling the business to get the
insights and the data they need

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:11.750
to deploy this at scale, using
it to deploy new intents,

00:28:11.750 --> 00:28:14.690
make changes on the fly when
it comes to the confidence

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:20.060
thresholds that we use,
changing the kind of language

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:25.220
that the virtual assistant
uses and that the customer sees

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:27.440
when they're communicating
with a virtual assistant

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:28.650
is really important to us.

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:32.750
And so being able to do that at
scale is really crucial for us.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:36.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

